WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Telemetry becomes much more useful when signals share consistent context.

2
00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:08.670
A metric that says latency increased is helpful.

3
00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:18.590
A metric that also includes the service, environment, cluster, namespace, workload, model version, deployment version, tenant class, and route is far more actionable.

4
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.440
Labels and identifiers turn isolated signals into an investigation path.

5
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:36.090
Important context may include service name, environment, cluster, region, namespace, node, workload, deployment version, model name, model version, request class, data source, tenant, request ID, trace ID, and correlation ID.

6
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.380
Not every signal needs every label.

7
00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:48.910
The design decision is to include the labels that help teams answer likely questions without creating uncontrolled cost, privacy, or cardinality problems.

8
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:58.050
Correlation helps teams connect a slow user request to a specific service, model deployment, GPU node, retrieval index, or downstream dependency.

9
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:07.580
When a request ID appears in logs, traces, events, and selected metrics, teams can move from symptom to cause with less guesswork.

10
00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:14.380
This is especially important in hybrid systems where ownership may cross teams and infrastructure boundaries.

11
00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:17.670
Too little metadata makes investigation slow.

12
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.520
Too much ungoverned metadata creates noise, cost, and privacy risk.

13
00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:29.320
Naming conventions, label discipline, and correlation rules should be treated as architecture decisions, not afterthoughts.

14
00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.370
Good context makes telemetry explainable.

15
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:39.560
It gives responders enough shared language to ask better questions during routine operations and during incidents.

